  In the chaos which follows, rumors abound. Foreign laborers are banding together with socialists to loot, rape, and kill.<K>
  Although these stories have little merit, a hurt, panicked people are ill-equipped to discern truth from fiction. Lynch mobs stalk the streets of Edo, claiming lives by the dozen.<K>
  However, the most famous murders of this period are committed not by hysterical townsfolk, but by soldiers acting with cold, clear purpose. And where some of these men, too, let paranoia drive them, still others see the rumors as merely an excuse to settle older grievances.<K>
  A few days after the earthquake, ten political activists are arrested, shot, and decapitated in the Katsushika district of Edo. This massacre, later called the Kameido Incident, is just the first of many.

