  September 16, Koryu 24. Osugi Sakae, his wife, and their two sons―ages six and seven―disappear on their way back to Edo from Yokohama, where they were visiting Sakae's sister, who had been injured in the quake.<K>
  One week later, the public learns what happened: that a group of radicals led by an Imperial Army captain abducted the family, executed them, then took their own lives.<K>
 
